## Migration Analysis

Your code has **3 patterns** that must change for .NET 8:

| API | Pattern | Replacement |
|-----|---------|-------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Cleanup on abort | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | `token.IsCancellationRequested` check |

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+ — there is no workaround. You must switch to **cooperative cancellation**.

---

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token so blocking can be interrupted
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (replaces ThreadAbortException catch)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — we control the loop via the while condition.
                // Loop exits naturally because token.IsCancellationRequested is true.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for clean exit (replaces Thread.Sleep + Abort)
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        _cts.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

---

## What Changed and Why

1. **`volatile bool _running` → `CancellationToken`** — The token integrates with blocking APIs (`WaitHandle`, async I/O) so the thread wakes up immediately instead of staying blocked until the next iteration.

2. **`Thread.Abort()` → `_cts.Cancel()`** — Requests cancellation cooperatively. No `PlatformNotSupportedException`.

3. **`catch (ThreadAbortException)` → `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)`** — The `when` guard ensures you only catch cancellation you initiated, not unrelated OCEs.

4. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` → removed** — Unnecessary. The loop condition (`!token.IsCancellationRequested`) controls exit naturally.

5. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` → `_workerThread.Join(2s)`** — Actually waits for the thread to finish rather than sleeping blindly.

6. **`_cts.Dispose()`** — `CancellationTokenSource` is `IDisposable`. Leaking it causes timer/callback leaks.

---

## Critical: You Must Update `GetNextItem`

If `GetNextItem()` blocks (e.g., on a queue or I/O), **cancellation stalls there** until it unblocks. You must pass the token through:

```csharp
// Before (blocks indefinitely — cancellation can't interrupt)
private Item GetNextItem()
{
    return _queue.Take(); // blocks until item available
}

// After (wakes up on cancellation)
private Item GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException on cancel
}
```

If it uses `Thread.Sleep`, replace with:
```csharp
// Before
Thread.Sleep(1000);

// After (wakes immediately on cancel)
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(1000);
```

---

## Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | What happens |
|---------|-------------|
| Not passing `token` to `GetNextItem` | Thread stays blocked after `Cancel()` — `Stop()` hangs or times out |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` and swallowing it | Hides cancellation from callers — let it propagate except at the top-level loop |
| Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource` | Timer and callback leaks in long-running services |
| Assuming cancellation is instant | It only fires at the next checkpoint — add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `ProcessItem` if it's long-running |
